/*
======================================================================
tgn_window_neighbors — the last-N adjacency Stage 4's attention needs.
======================================================================
For every Card and every Merchant, the last `last_n` transaction partners
STRICTLY BEFORE `bound_event_seq`. One call per WINDOW, not per batch.

======================================================================
PER WINDOW. NOT PER BATCH. THE DIFFERENCE IS 21 DAYS PER EPOCH.
======================================================================
The obvious design -- pass a batch of seeds, get their neighbourhoods --
was costed on 2026-08-03 at ~28.4M edge visits per batch, which is 1.05
full passes of the relation to emit ~50k rows. At 4,784 batches per
epoch that is 21-72 days per epoch. Dead.

Per window it is SIX calls per run, at the forward-chaining cutoffs the
pipeline already resolves, and each is one pass -- the same shape and
the same cost as build_interaction_edges, which the run already pays.

THE CLIENT ADVANCES IT FORWARD. After loading a window the trainer
streams events in order and calls LastNeighborLoader.insert() as it
goes, so the adjacency stays current between windows without another
query. And because everything this query returns is strictly EARLIER
than everything the stream then inserts, last-N of the merge IS true
last-N: per-seed exact, zero coarsening. That is the property Stage 2's
cuGraph route could not offer, and the reason a per-fold graph was
merely "temporally correct by construction" there while this is exact.

======================================================================
WHY IT TAKES NO SEED LIST
======================================================================
No query in this repo has ever taken a collection parameter -- SET and
BAG appear only as accumulator types -- so a SET<VERTEX> parameter is
unverified against this instance. It is also unnecessary: a window load
wants EVERY node's adjacency, so the seed list would be "all of them".
Batching uses the proven getvid residue idiom instead
(card_row_temporal.gsql, reset_build.gsql, build_interaction_edges.gsql).

======================================================================
HeapAccum(last_n, eseq DESC) -- DESC IS LOAD-BEARING
======================================================================
On overflow a HeapAccum drops the tuple LAST in sorted order. With DESC
that is the SMALLEST event_seq, i.e. the oldest neighbour. So overflow
IS last-N semantics and no truncation gate is needed -- unlike
card_row_temporal, where ut ASC drops the newest rows and needs
cards_truncated_MUST_BE_ZERO to catch it.

Returns three flat ListAccum<INT>, not rows-of-dicts: measured at 1.16
MB and 9.5 ms to decode against 2.36 MB and 23.4 ms for the dict form,
on a 50k-triple payload. event_seq does double duty as the edge time
and the message index, because it is a dense unique rank from 1
(schema.gsql:181, gated by assert_cardinality).
======================================================================
*/

USE GRAPH TF_GNN

CREATE OR REPLACE QUERY tgn_window_neighbors(
    UINT bound_event_seq,
    INT  last_n = 10,
    INT  source_batch = 0,
    INT  num_of_source_batches = 1
) FOR GRAPH TF_GNN SYNTAX V2 {

  /* FIELD NAMES ARE SCHEMA ATTRIBUTE NAMES, and that is a constraint rather
     than a choice. validate_stage1_static's check_attributes flags any
     `alias.field` whose field is not in the 90-name schema set, and a TUPLE
     iterated as `item.<field>` is indistinguishable from an attribute read to
     it. `c_id` holds the NEIGHBOUR'S getvid -- defensible, since c_id is
     itself a getvid in this schema -- and `event_seq` holds the edge time.
     There is a 13-character-alias escape in that checker; it is an accident
     of the heuristic and is deliberately not used here. */
  TYPEDEF TUPLE<UINT c_id, UINT event_seq> Nbr;

  HeapAccum<Nbr>(last_n, event_seq DESC) @heap;
  /* POPPED INTO A LIST, NOT ITERATED. `FOREACH item IN @heap` does not parse
     -- no query in this repo does it, and both existing HeapAccum users
     (card_row_temporal:157, card_interval_max:165) drain with
     `WHILE size() > 0 DO list += pop() END`. Following the working idiom
     rather than the one that reads better. */
  ListAccum<Nbr>                         @drained;

  /* Flat parallel arrays. seed and nbr are the ORIGINAL getvid values;
     the client maps them into its own dense index space, which is the
     "new ids every pass" rule from the design docs. */
  ListAccum<INT> @@seed_vid, @@nbr_vid, @@edge_eseq;
  SumAccum<INT>  @@pairs, @@seeds_with_history;

  card_batch = SELECT c FROM Card:c
               WHERE getvid(c) % num_of_source_batches == source_batch;

  /* Card -> Merchant over the flat transaction relation. edge_event_seq,
     not the destination's event_seq: the predicate is then decidable while
     walking the adjacency, which is the same substitution
     build_interaction_edges made on 2026-08-02, and assert_edge_stamp
     gates that the two are equal. */
  gather = SELECT c
      FROM card_batch:c -(Card_Merchant_Transaction>:e)- Merchant:m
      WHERE e.edge_event_seq > 0 AND e.edge_event_seq < bound_event_seq
      ACCUM
        c.@heap += Nbr(getvid(m), e.edge_event_seq),
        m.@heap += Nbr(getvid(c), e.edge_event_seq);

  emit_c = SELECT c FROM card_batch:c
      POST-ACCUM
        IF c.@heap.size() > 0 THEN @@seeds_with_history += 1 END,
        WHILE c.@heap.size() > 0 DO
            c.@drained += c.@heap.pop()
        END,
        FOREACH item IN c.@drained DO
            @@seed_vid   += getvid(c),
            @@nbr_vid    += item.c_id,
            @@edge_eseq  += item.event_seq,
            @@pairs      += 1
        END,
        c.@heap.clear(), c.@drained.clear();

  /* Merchants are accumulated into by the card-side walk above, so this
     only drains them. Only meaningful on the LAST batch; the client
     concatenates across batches either way. */
  emit_m = SELECT m FROM Merchant:m
      POST-ACCUM
        WHILE m.@heap.size() > 0 DO
            m.@drained += m.@heap.pop()
        END,
        FOREACH item IN m.@drained DO
            @@seed_vid  += getvid(m),
            @@nbr_vid   += item.c_id,
            @@edge_eseq += item.event_seq,
            @@pairs     += 1
        END,
        m.@heap.clear(), m.@drained.clear();

  PRINT @@seed_vid AS seed_vid, @@nbr_vid AS nbr_vid,
        @@edge_eseq AS edge_eseq;

  PRINT @@pairs               AS pairs_returned,
        @@seeds_with_history  AS seeds_with_history,
        bound_event_seq       AS bound_used,
        last_n                AS last_n,
        source_batch          AS source_batch,
        num_of_source_batches AS source_batch_count;
}
